          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Who does guidance describe as a priority group for influenza vaccination?",
          "answer": "The guidance I found does not single out priority groups for flu vaccination. It says routine annual vaccination is recommended for everyone aged 6 months and older who has no contraindication, rather than only for high-risk groups. This comes from the influenza guidance (*Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment*). The same document says vaccination before the end of October is the usual aim in the northern hemisphere, and that later vaccination is still worthwhile while the virus is circulating.\n\nTwo other documents mention flu vaccination in the context of specific conditions:\n\n- The coronary artery disease guidance (*secondary prevention after a diagnosis*) lists annual influenza vaccination as a cardiovascular measure for people with that condition.\n- The COPD guidance (*spirometric diagnosis, group assessment and initial therapy*) says vaccination against influenza reduces flare-ups.\n\nThese passages do not say whether they are describing special priority groups or simply reinforcing the general recommendation. The influenza document has a section on disagreements, but the passage I received did not include it, so I can't tell you what that disagreement covers.\n\nIf you have a specific health condition or question about your own vaccination, a clinician or pharmacist can advise you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
